    "reasoning": "All 16 stated final answers are mathematically correct and supported by their own captured solutions. However, q-128309 contains an authored intermediate sign error: its cofactor expansion requires +1(-3-20), whereas one line prints -1(-3-20). The subsequent calculation and final answer -32 are correct. Thus q-128309 has a source working error without disagreement about the answer; the other 15 questions are confirmed. Independently: q-11637 has CD=DC=I and DE≠I; q-102674 has AB=BA=I and AC≠I; q-71879 has BC=CB=I but (AB)12=-1; q-149832 has AC=CA=I but (AB)11=2. The determinants in q-128309 and q-38180 are -8-1-23=-32 and -6-6=-12. For q-128491, det(S)=28-5=23, so only II is true. For q-128480, det(D)=-22+21+1=0, so I and III are true. In q-9913, singularity requires 3k(k-2)=0, giving k=0,2. In q-124410, 3!-0!=6-1=5. The inverse formula gives the displayed inverses for q-127736 and q-127737, whose determinants are 2 and 1 respectively. In q-127755, A is rectangular and C has determinant zero, while det(B)=1. In q-7915, P and Q are square with zero determinant; rectangular R is not called singular under the stated convention. Finally, q-71969 requires 6t=0, and q-11719 requires 9-t²=0, giving t=0 and t=±3 respectively.",
